---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Terms of use — viewbus";
const description =
  "The licence terms for viewbus: what you may do with it, what it does not promise, and where liability stops.";
const path = "/terms";

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://viewbus.app/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Terms of use", item: "https://viewbus.app/terms/" },
  ],
};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path={path}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
  </Fragment>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-20 prose">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">Terms of use</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-12">Last updated: 2026-07-26</p>

    <p>
      viewbus is free software in the "costs nothing" sense, not the open-source sense: the source is
      closed, but you may use it for anything, including commercial work, without paying or signing up.
      These terms cover the desktop app, this website, and the JSON feeds served from
      <code>viewbus.app</code>. Installing or using viewbus means you accept them. They're written to be
      read, not to be impressive.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Who you're dealing with</h2>
    <p>
      viewbus is made and operated by <strong>Håkon Lia</strong>, an individual developer based in
      Norway. There is no company, no support contract, and no service-level agreement. Reach me through
      the routes on the <a href="/support/">support page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">What you may do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Install and run viewbus on as many machines as you like, personally or at work.</li>
      <li>Use it against production systems, at your own discretion and risk.</li>
      <li>Build tools on the documented <a href="/developers/">local HTTP API</a> and the built-in MCP server, and distribute those tools freely.</li>
      <li>Write and share themes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">What you may not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Reverse-engineer, decompile, or disassemble the app, except where that right can't be excluded by law.</li>
      <li>Redistribute modified builds, or distribute viewbus as though it were yours.</li>
      <li>Sell viewbus, or bundle it into a paid product as a headline feature, without asking me first.</li>
      <li>Use it to access systems you aren't authorised to access.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Your data and your Azure resources</h2>
    <p>
      viewbus talks to Azure with your credentials, on your machine. You are responsible for the access
      you grant it and for what you do with that access. Some operations are destructive by
      design — purging a queue, emptying a dead-letter queue, bulk-deleting messages — and those actions
      are permanent. viewbus asks for confirmation, but it will do exactly what you tell it to. Verify
      the target resource before you confirm, and keep your own backups of anything you can't lose.
    </p>
    <p>
      viewbus has no servers and stores nothing about you. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>
      lists every file it writes and every host it contacts.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">No warranty</h2>
    <p>
      viewbus is provided <strong>"as is"</strong>, without warranty of any kind, express or implied,
      including but not limited to warranties of merchantability, fitness for a particular purpose, and
      non-infringement. I don't promise that it is free of defects, that it will suit your setup, that
      it will keep working after an Azure API change, or that any given release will be available
      indefinitely.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Limitation of liability</h2>
    <p>
      To the fullest extent permitted by law, I am not liable for any indirect, incidental, special,
      consequential, or exemplary damages, nor for lost profits, lost revenue, lost data, deleted
      messages, or business interruption, arising from your use of or inability to use viewbus — even if
      I've been told such damages are possible. Where liability cannot be excluded, it is limited to the
      amount you paid for viewbus, which is zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here limits liability for wilful misconduct or gross negligence, or any other liability
      that can't be limited under Norwegian law. If you're a consumer, your mandatory statutory rights
      are unaffected.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Third-party services</h2>
    <p>
      viewbus connects to Microsoft Azure using your own credentials. Your use of Azure is governed by
      your agreement with Microsoft, not by these terms, and Azure charges — including request charges
      generated by viewbus peeking or listing resources — are between you and Microsoft. viewbus is not
      affiliated with, sponsored by, or endorsed by Microsoft. Distribution through the Microsoft Store
      or Homebrew is additionally subject to those platforms' own terms.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Updates and availability</h2>
    <p>
      The app checks <code>viewbus.app/latest.json</code> on startup and can install updates you accept;
      Microsoft Store installs update through the Store. I may change, break, or discontinue any feature,
      and I may stop publishing viewbus entirely. Copies you've already installed keep working — nothing
      phones home for permission to run.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Changes to these terms</h2>
    <p>
      If these terms change, the date at the top changes with them, and material changes will be called
      out in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>. Continuing to use viewbus after a change means you
      accept the new version.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-3">Governing law</h2>
    <p>
      These terms are governed by the laws of Norway, without regard to conflict-of-law rules. If you're
      a consumer resident elsewhere in the EEA, you keep the protection of the mandatory rules of your
      own country.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
